Dear  Kristijan,

A quick update on my previous post. I apologize, as I made a mistake in my previous message when commenting on the StackExchange post. After reviewing it carefully, Terrence Jorgensen’s suggestion to modify the baseline model so that it is actually nested in the model with equality-constrained thresholds was intended to obtain valid incremental fit indices (CFI and TLI). Therefore, it appears that this would not solve the problem of obtaining NA values for the robust fit indices in models with thresholds constrained to equality, but it would allow for the correct comparative fit indices (scaled, not robust) to be obtained between the configurational model and the more restricted model.

Another much more relevant point that I’ve just realized is that your model has binary items. Remember that, according to the guidelines by Wu and Estabrook (2016), with binary items there is no valid way to test the equality of thresholds, loadings, and intercepts separately. Therefore, measurement invariance in a model with binary indicators is limited to simultaneously constraining the thresholds, loadings, and intercepts (while leaving the residual variance of the items freely estimated) to test for strong invariance (against configural invariance). If strong invariance is violated, it could be due to any of these parameters (thresholds, loadings or intercepts), and the data do not allow us to distinguish between the sources of the invariance violation.
In any case, since your goal is to demonstrate the invariance of the scale you are developing, because it contains binary items, you can only test for the presence of scalar invariance, and, as far as I know, the best approach is still to perform the likelihood ratio test rather than relying on differences in the fit indices between the models; therefore, I wouldn’t be too concerned about the NA values in the robust fit indices either.
Another very appropriate option for studying the invariance of your scale would be to use random permutation tests applied to measurement invariance with categorical items using the “permuteMeasEq” function from the semTools package, as it allows for better control of Type I error rates (https://doi.org/10.1080/10705511. 2017.1421467 <https://doi.org/10.1080/10705511.%202017.1421467>).
Hope it helps!

Best regards,
Adrián García

